  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday Night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1115 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nThe front will continue to make progress southward through \nWednesday night and into Thursday with showers and thunderstorms \nspreading over much of E OK and NW AR. The synoptic front will \ntend to stall over the area Thursday, though outflow from \nthunderstorms may extend well into SE OK. This will complicate the\ntemperature forecast, with any areas that remain undisturbed \nlikely still achieving well above average values. Behind the \nfront, and especially in areas which receive meaningful rainfall,\ntemperatures will be significantly cooler than previous \ndays...likely struggling to warm out of the 80s. The current \ntemperature forecast favors less influence from the front and \nassociated convection over SE OK and W-Central AR, with highs \nstill in the mid-upper 90s. If this verifies, additional heat \nheadlines may be required. With that said, several CAMs show \npotential for outflow/cooler temperatures to encompass the entire \nCWA, and this evolution will need to be watched closely. \nThunderstorms Thursday afternoon may produce gusty winds, \nespecially with any clustering that occurs, though severe weather \nappears less likely as of now due to poor wind shear.\n\nThe front lifts back to the north as a shortwave moves through \nthe region on Friday. This will prolong low precipitation chances \nacross the area, with SE OK and NW AR being most favored for \nafternoon thunderstorms (20-50 percent chance). Otherwise, drier \nweather is forecast on Friday with lessening cloud cover. As a \nresult, temperatures will recover back into the 90s for the entire\narea, with heat indices in the lower 100s possible again. \nMeanwhile, a shortwave trough moving across the northern plains \nwill send another cold front southward Friday night into Saturday,\npotentially bringing additional rain chances to portions of the \narea, particularly along and north of I-40. Yet another storm \nsystem is forecast to move through the central states early next \nweek, bringing another front near or into the CWA with additional \nrainfall potential.\n\nTemperatures this weekend into next week will be largely driven \nby frontal placement and resulting precipitation/cloud coverage. \nModel spread increases significantly by the end of the forecast \nperiod with differences of up to 15-20 degrees noted between the \nNBM 25th and 75th percentile. The current temperature forecast \nleans more towards status quo, with highs in the 90s persisting \ninto next week. This is better than our latest streak of 100+ \nweather...but still several degrees above average for mid \nSeptember. If the fronts can make more meaningful intrusions into\nour area, cooler temperatures can be expected.",
